WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
In the lecture’s simplified collapse budget, about ninety

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.480
nine percent of the energy escapes in neutrinos.

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:08.520
About one percent remains for other channels. That

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.760
remainder is not the same as visible light.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:13.880
Follow the escaping particles separately from the expanding,

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.000
energized gas.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:19.230
The first hot fireball follows shock heating. Later,

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:22.450
radioactive nuclei deposit more energy as they decay.

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:25.790
Nickel fifty six changes into cobalt fifty six,

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:29.290
then iron fifty six. This decay chain can

00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:31.810
sustain the fading light; calling this process fission

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:33.930
confuses two different nuclear mechanisms.

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:38.590
This moving reconstruction illustrates selected physical relationships. A

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:41.610
numerical simulation instead evolves a stated model from

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:45.150
equations and initial conditions. Neither realistic artwork nor

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:48.470
a powerful computer guarantees the right explanation. Compare

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:51.210
the computed predictions with observations, and keep the

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:52.570
model’s assumptions visible.
